,"text":"Index"},{"kind":"text","text":">?"}],"type":"topic","title":"hamiltonianCycle()","abstract":[{"text":"A Hamiltonian cycle, or nil (Mathematica ","type":"text"},{"code":"FindHamiltonianCycle","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"): the search from the first","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"vertex, closed by the first unused edge of the last vertex’s row back to it. It leaves the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"first vertex through the lesser of its two edges in that vertex’s row (the search meets","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"that orientation first), as Cycles’ canonical form does. A self-loop is the cycle of a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"one-vertex graph and two parallel edges the cycle of a two-vertex one (the ","type":"text"},{"code":"Cycle","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" type’s","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"rules).","type":"text"}],"kind":"symbol"},"doc://Tours/documentation/Tours/GraphProtocols/Graph/hamiltonianPath()":{"role":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/tours\/graphprotocols\/graph\/hamiltonianpath()","identifier":"doc:\/\/Tours\/documentation\/Tours\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/hamiltonianPath()","fragments":[{"text":"func","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"hamiltonianPath","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"() -> ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Path","preciseIdentifier":"s:5Walks4PathV","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"text":"Self","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":".","kind":"text"},{"text":"Vertex","preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P6VertexQa","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":", ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Self","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":".","kind":"text"},{"text":"Edges","preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P5EdgesQa","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":".","kind":"text"},{"text":"Index","preciseIdentifier":"s:Sl5IndexQa","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":">?","kind":"text"}],"type":"topic","title":"hamiltonianPath()","kind":"symbol","abstract":[{"text":"A Hamiltonian path, or nil when there is none (or no vertex): the first one a depth-first","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"backtracking search finds, from each vertex in ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertices"},{"text":" order, extending through each","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"vertex’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"incidentEdges"},{"text":" in order to an unvisited vertex (Mathematica","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"FindHamiltonianPath"},{"text":"). Self-loops never help; one vertex is the trivial path. Pruning cuts","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"only subtrees with no solution (an unvisited vertex unreachable, or left with too few","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"usable neighbours), so it keeps the order. Without a start the search is run again from","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"each vertex in turn until one succeeds, so a graph with no Hamiltonian path costs n","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"searches.","type":"text"}]},"doc://Tours/documentation/Tours/GraphProtocols/Graph/eulerianTrail()":{"title":"eulerianTrail()","url":"\/documentation\/tours\/graphprotocols\/graph\/euleriantrail()","type":"topic","abstract":[{"text":"An Eulerian trail (NetworkX ","type":"text"},{"code":"eulerian_path","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", igraph ","type":"text"},{"code":"igraph_eulerian_path","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"), or nil when","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"there is none or the graph has no vertex. It starts at the first odd vertex in ","type":"text"},{"code":"vertices","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"order, or, when the graph is Eulerian, at the first vertex with an edge (the circuit as a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"closed trail); it is the trivial trail at the first ver